---
title: "Evaluate agents"
description: "Score agent runs against the same task definitions in CI and against collected production records, with run_eval and the wyrd eval CLI."
---

Model evaluation compares an output to a label. Agent evaluation is harder: the
final response is one artifact of a process that also includes tool calls,
multi-turn dialogue, and intermediate scores that matter independently of
whether the final answer looks right.

Wyrd scores an agent run with the same task definitions in two places — a
multi-turn scenario the server drives (CI / pre-deploy), and a batch of records
you collected during inference (offline analysis). You write the tasks once.

## The shipped surface

Two entry points exist today, both backed by the same task engine:

- **`wyrd.eval.run_eval`** — a Python client that drives a server-run scenario
  over a pull protocol. Your agent answers turns; the server scores the
  completed interaction.
- **`wyrd eval run`** — the CLI, with a server-protocol mode and a local-records
  mode for pre-collected `EvalRecordObservation` rows.

The Eval card itself (tasks, scenarios, dataset) is registered like any other
card. See [Register cards](/server/register-cards/).

## Drive a scenario from Python

`run_eval` opens a run on the server, then loops: the server asks for the next
turn, your `agent_fn` produces a response, and the server scores the completed
scenario. The signature is exactly:

```python
from wyrd.eval import run_eval, ConversationTurn, AgentTurnResponse


def agent_fn(message: str, history: list[ConversationTurn]) -> AgentTurnResponse:
    # `history` turns carry role "user" or "agent" (lowercase) and content.
    reply = my_agent.respond(message, history)
    return {
        "response": reply.text,
        # Optional EvalRecordObservation rows for mechanic-view tasks.
        "records": reply.eval_records,
    }


summary = run_eval(
    "http://127.0.0.1:8080",
    "my-team/support-eval@1.0.0",
    agent_fn,
)

print(summary["run_id"], summary["server_url"])
```

`agent_fn` may return a bare `str` instead of an `AgentTurnResponse` when you
have no records to attach. `run_eval` returns a `RunSummary` —
`{"run_id": ..., "server_url": ...}` — once the server emits `RunComplete`.

<Aside title="Role casing" data-variant="wyrd">

`ConversationTurn["role"]` is `"user"` or `"agent"` — **lowercase**. This is the
wire contract the server sends and expects; uppercase roles are rejected.

</Aside>

### Simulated users

Non-scripted user turns come from the server by default. To supply them from the
client instead, pass `simulated_user="client"` and a `simulated_user_fn`:

```python
def simulated_user_fn(history: list[ConversationTurn]) -> str:
    return next_user_message(history)


run_eval(
    "http://127.0.0.1:8080",
    "my-team/support-eval@1.0.0",
    agent_fn,
    simulated_user="client",
    simulated_user_fn=simulated_user_fn,
)
```

If the server requests a client-delegated user turn and no `simulated_user_fn`
was supplied, `run_eval` raises a `WyrdError`.

## Drive a scenario from the CLI

`wyrd eval run` takes an Eval card and one source — a server to drive, or a
JSONL file of pre-collected records:

```bash
# Server-protocol mode: the CLI drives a scenario against a running agent.
wyrd eval run \
  --eval eval-cards/support-eval.yaml \
  --server http://127.0.0.1:8080 \
  --agent-url http://127.0.0.1:9000/agent

# Local-records mode: score a JSONL batch of EvalRecordObservation rows.
wyrd eval run \
  --eval eval-cards/support-eval.yaml \
  --records runs/2026-06-29.jsonl
```

`--server` and `--records` are mutually exclusive; exactly one is required.
Useful flags:

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--simulated-user server\|client` | Who supplies unscripted user turns. Default `server`. |
| `--simulated-user-script <PATH>` | JSONL of client-supplied user turns (with `--simulated-user client`). |
| `--baseline <PATH>` | Compare against a prior `EvalResults` JSON. |
| `--out <DIR>` | Write local result artifacts (`WYRD_EVAL_OUT`). |
| `--judge-mock` | Force all LLM-judge tasks to pass. Smoke-test wiring only — results are not real. |
| `--fail-on-gate` | Exit non-zero when the pass gate fails. Default on, for CI. |

## What the engine scores

Tasks are what the engine evaluates against each record. They compose in a DAG:
cheap deterministic checks first, expensive judge calls only when their
dependencies pass.

| Task | What it checks | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| `AssertionTask` | Deterministic rules: values, thresholds, formats, patterns | Zero — pure Rust |
| `LlmJudgeTask` | Semantic quality via a registered `Prompt` card | One LLM call per record |
| `TraceAssertionTask` | OTel span properties: order, duration, retries, token budgets | Zero |
| `AgentAssertionTask` | Tool calls and response structure: which tools ran, with what args | Zero |

```yaml
tasks:
  response_not_empty:
    kind: assertion
    id: response_not_empty
    context_path: "$.response"
    operator: length_greater_than
    expected: 10

  helpfulness:
    kind: llm_judge
    id: helpfulness
    judge_ref:
      kind: Prompt
      name: helpfulness-judge
      version: 1.0.0
      space: my-team
    context_path: "$.score"
    operator: greater_than_or_equals
    expected: 4
    depends_on: [response_not_empty]
```

`depends_on` creates a topological dependency: `helpfulness` only runs when
`response_not_empty` passes. Tasks in the same stage run concurrently; each stage
extends the snapshot, so downstream tasks can JSONPath into
`$.task.<id>.passed`, `$.task.<id>.actual`, and `$.task.<id>.parsed`.

### Passenger and mechanic views

A passenger only cares whether the car got from A to B; a mechanic cares whether
the engine ran correctly even if it did. Wyrd scores both from one run:

- **Passenger** — did the agent produce the right final response? Tasks run
  against `$.response`.
- **Mechanic** — did the run stay healthy underneath? Tasks run against the
  `EvalRecordObservation` rows your `agent_fn` attached (or the JSONL you fed
  `--records`): tool calls, trace spans, intermediate scores.

A passing final response does not imply passing mechanic tasks. A scenario
passes only when both do.

## Not yet shipped

Always-on online monitoring — an agent emitting eval records continuously while
the server scores them asynchronously in the background — is **not a shipped
surface**. Today you attach records to an `agent_fn` response or collect them
into a JSONL batch and score it with `wyrd eval run --records`. See the
[Roadmap](/roadmap/).

